After you install macOS 27 Golden Gate, Apple automatically downloads a multi-gigabyte AI model to your Mac the next time it's connected to the internet. Unlike previous versions of macOS, you can't prevent it. Siri AI, and by extension Apple Intelligence, are now a mandatory part of your system.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2024/09/apple-intelligence-black.jpeg" alt="" width="1400" height="788" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-963902" /><br/>
For macOS 27 Golden Gate, Apple says the latest Apple Intelligence features require up to 14GB of on-device storage on Macs with an M3 chip or later and at least 12GB of unified memory, and up to 8GB of storage for other Apple Intelligence-enabled Macs. But in our experience, AI model storage allocation appears to be considerably higher on some Macs than others.<br/>
<br/>
On an M4 Pro Mac mini running macOS 27, for example, <em>MacRumors</em> saw 20.67GB handed over for Apple Intelligence, according to the Storage section in System Settings. <em><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/09/macos-27-golden-gate-the-ars-technica-review/">ArsTechnica</a></em> recorded an even higher figure of 22.42GB on an M3 MacBook Air after a fresh installation of macOS 27.0 beta 7. Both of these systems will be using Apple's <a href="https://machinelearning.apple.com/research/introducing-third-generation-of-apple-foundation-models">AFM 3 Core Advanced</a> on-device model, which requires an M3-or-newer Mac with 12GB or more of RAM.<br/>
<br/>
This doesn't explain why Macs that don't meet the minimum requirements for Apple's most advanced model are still losing unusually large amounts of storage to Apple Intelligence. <em>ArsTechnica</em> reported the same screen showed around 14GB on an M1 MacBook Air and a MacBook Neo, and yet both of these models fall into a category for which Apple lists a requirement of up to 8GB.<br/>
<br/>
Other Mac users have posted similar higher-than-expected readings. For example, one Reddit user <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/MacOSBeta/comments/1we9raa/its_just_a_beta_what_do_you_expect_they_said/">shared</a> a screenshot from the macOS 27 Release Candidate showing Apple Intelligence gobbled up 30.16GB on their machine. Another user <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/apple/comments/1wi1caq/macos_27_golden_gate_the_ars_technica_review/">quoted</a> 24.35GB for Apple Intelligence after installing macOS 27 on a MacBook Pro with M3 Pro chip.<br/>
